        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Impressionism</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Where</span><span class="info-stat-val"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, above all <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, its cafés, boulevards and the studio of the photographer Nadar, and the countryside where the painters worked in the open air; later shown in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> and taken up across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a></span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val">the core painters meet in the early 1860s; Salon des Refusés 1863; Société anonyme founded December 1873; first exhibition April 1874; eight group exhibitions 1874–1886; Post-Impressionism from the 1880s</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Leading figures</span><span class="info-stat-val"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/claude-monet">Claude Monet</a>, the most prolific and stereotypical Impressionist; Pierre-Auguste Renoir; Alfred Sisley; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camille-pissarro">Camille Pissarro</a>, the only one to show at all eight exhibitions; Berthe Morisot; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edgar-degas">Edgar Degas</a>, who despised the term; Paul Cézanne; Mary Cassatt; Gustave Caillebotte; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edouard-manet">Édouard Manet</a>, regarded as their leader though he never exhibited with them</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Outcome</span><span class="info-stat-val">few financial rewards from the exhibitions, but gradual public acceptance; by the early 1880s diluted Impressionist methods were commonplace in Salon art, and the movement became a precursor of Post-Impressionism, Fauvism and Cubism</span></div>
        </div>
