    <section class="kid-tale" aria-labelledby="kid-tale-title">
      <h2 class="kid-tale-title" id="kid-tale-title">The story of Impressionism</h2>
      <ol class="kid-tale-pages"><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/napoleon-iii.jpg" alt="Napoleon III" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Napoleon III <span class="kid-tale-cr">Alexandre Cabanel, Public domain</span></figcaption></figure><p>About 150 years ago in France, an art academy liked smooth, careful paintings of history, religion and important people.</p><p>When its judges turned many paintings away, Emperor Napoleon III let the public see them at a show of their own.</p></li><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ukiyo-e.jpg" alt="Ukiyo-e" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Ukiyo-e <span class="kid-tale-cr">Sharaku, Public domain</span></figcaption></figure><p>New things helped young painters: paint in tin tubes, bright new colours, and Japanese prints with unusual angles.</p></li><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/photography.jpg" alt="Photography" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Photography <span class="kid-tale-cr">Joseph Nicéphore Niépce, Public domain</span></figcaption></figure><p>Shut out by the judges, the painters held their own shows, the first in the studio of a photographer named Nadar.</p><p>Women painters such as Berthe Morisot and Mary Cassatt took part, though they faced heavy limits.</p></li><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/impressionism.jpg" alt="Impressionism" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Impressionism <span class="kid-tale-cr">Claude Monet, Public domain</span></figcaption></figure><p>They painted outdoors to catch the passing sunlight, with short strokes of pure colour side by side.</p><p>A critic made fun of Claude Monet’s painting Impression, Sunrise, and the name “Impressionists” stuck.</p></li><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/vincent-van-gogh.jpg" alt="Vincent van Gogh" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Vincent van Gogh <span class="kid-tale-cr">Vincent van Gogh, Public domain</span></figcaption></figure><p>At first many people attacked their work, but slowly the public came to see it as fresh and new.</p><p>It opened the way for later painters like Vincent van Gogh, and spread as far as America, Australia and Japan.</p></li></ol>
    </section>

    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Impressionist painters worked in the open air to catch the passing effects of sunlight, laying short, broken strokes of pure colour side by side instead of the smooth, blended finish the Académie demanded.</li><li>Shut out by the Salon jury, they founded their own society in December 1873 and held eight independent exhibitions from 1874 to 1886; a critic’s mocking review of the first gave them their name.</li><li>First met with harsh opposition, the style gradually won public acceptance and opened the way to Post-Impressionism, Fauvism and Cubism, with echoes in music, literature, sculpture and film.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The name came from a joke: in a satirical review of the 1874 exhibition in Le Charivari, the critic Louis Leroy played on the title of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/claude-monet">Claude Monet</a>’s Impression, Sunrise and wrote that wallpaper in its embryonic state was more finished than that seascape.</p></aside>
    </section>

